Faceted Flight: 10th Anniversary Edition: licensing at a glance
================================================================

This repository is licensed in two halves, and a third category is neither
half. Read this before reusing anything.


CODE: MIT (see LICENSE)
    The three.js + WebXR game engine and everything built with it: src/,
    tools/, tests/, functions/, workers/, migrations/, .githooks/, and the
    build configuration.

    GitHub labels the repository from that file. The label describes the CODE
    only.


CONTENT: CC BY-NC 4.0 (see CONTENT-LICENSE.md)
    The game's first-party creative work: the level designs and gameplay data
    in content/, the flight tuning in src/generated/, the recorded flights in
    content/ghosts/, and the first-party models, terrain maps and fonts in the
    open content pack.

    This is a deliberate split. A mission's ring layout is design work
    expressed as numbers, and a permissive code license would let anyone ship
    those missions commercially. You may remix, learn from and build
    non-commercial projects on the content, with credit. For a commercial
    grant, open an issue and ask.


THIRD-PARTY MATERIAL WITH ITS OWN TERMS (see THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md)
    three.js (MIT), the Bitter and Roboto Condensed typefaces (SIL Open Font
    License 1.1), the CRYSTAL typeface (CC BY-SA 4.0) and six music tracks by
    HoliznaCC0 (CC0 1.0). Each keeps its own license, which may be stricter or
    looser than the two above. THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md lists them, with versions.

    The live game at facetedflight.net also uses 3D models from commercial
    asset packs, under licenses that allow use inside a finished game and do
    not allow handing the models to someone else. NONE of that material is in
    this repository or in the content pack, and none of it may be added. The
    denylist in .githooks/check-denylist.mjs enforces that on every commit.
    A clone of this repository runs without those models.


NAME AND BRANDING: not licensed
    "Faceted Flight", "Uncle Matt Makes", "The Department of Silly Stuff", and
    the game's logo and branding identify this project and its author. Forks
    and derived games must ship under their own name and branding. Code and
    content may be used under the terms above; the name and the look of the
    brand may not.


